window.docContents[134].resolve({"/#Jakarta-Persistence--3___-Entity-Operations--3___6___-Entity-Instance-Lifecycle--3___6___1___-Lifecycle-Operations":{"contents":"The fundamental operations of an EntityManager are:\n\n* persist() to make a new entity persistent, as defined in Section 3.6.3,* remove(), to remove a persistent entity from the database, as defined in Section 3.6.4,* flush(), to force synchronization of pending modifications with the database, as defined in Section 3.6.6,* refresh(), to reload an entity from the database, as defined in Section 3.6.7,* detach(), to disassociate an entity from its persistence context, as defined in Section 3.6.8,* merge(), to copy the state of a detached instance into the persistence context, and* lock() to obtain a lock an entity instance or to upgrade the lock level currently held, as defined in Section 3.9.\n\nThe methods persist(), remove(), refresh(), detach(), and merge() are called lifecycle operations.\n\nThe cascade annotation element of a relationship modeling annotation, as defined in Section 2.11, specifies which lifecycle operations propagate to associated entities. Each lifecycle operation is identified by the corresponding member of the enumeration CascadeType.\n\npublic enum CascadeType {ALL, PERSIST, MERGE, REMOVE, REFRESH, DETACH}\n\n\nThe value cascade=ALL is equivalent to cascade={PERSIST, MERGE, REMOVE, REFRESH, DETACH}.\n\nNoteThe cascade functionality is most typically used to model parent-child relationships. A closely related feature is orphan removal, which is defined below in Section 3.6.5.\n\nInformation about how lifecycle operations interact with transactions may be found below in Section 3.7.2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Jakarta Persistence\u00093. Entity Operations\u00093.6. Entity Instance Lifecycle","header":"3.6.1. Lifecycle Operations","id":"/#Jakarta-Persistence--3___-Entity-Operations--3___6___-Entity-Instance-Lifecycle--3___6___1___-Lifecycle-Operations"}});